// The exponent of a float constant (`2.0e10`, `1.0e-30`, `1.5E+3`, which is how
// the compiler spells a literal with an exponent in the IR it prints), and the
// scaling it asks for. The result is exact when the digits fit 2^53 and the
// net power of ten is at most 22, because then one multiplication or division
// by an exactly representable power rounds once, as strtod does; beyond that
// the power is built by repeated multiplication and the last bits can differ
// from the compiled targets, which hand the text to a real strtod.

// The exponent at the cursor: `e` or `E`, an optional sign, digits; 0 if the
// cursor is not at one.
ld_exp() {
  int c = ld_at();
  int ex = 0;
  if (c == 101 || c == 69) {
    ld_adv(1);
    int pl = ld_eat(43);
    ex = ld_num();
  }
} return int ex;
given tf_ld("e10") ():(10) then (import ld_pr)[(import pr_p)]:(3)
